 {
  "id": "CS-001",
  "book_id": "DEMO-04",
  "type": "concept",
  "topic": "Data structures",
  "stage": "tech",
  "difficulty": 1,
  "difficulty_source": "authored",
  "question": "What is a hash map, and why is looking up a key usually fast?",
  "model_answer": "A hash map stores key-value pairs. A hash function turns the key into a position in an array, so a lookup jumps straight there instead of scanning. It is fast on average, constant time, because keys spread out. Two keys can land in the same place (a collision), which is handled by keeping a small list per slot or probing for another slot. With many collisions the lookup slows down.",
  "go_deeper": "Listen for average versus worst case, and for the idea that a good hash function spreads keys evenly.",
  "key_points": [
   {
    "point": "A hash function turns the key into an array position",
    "weight": 5
   },
   {
    "point": "Lookup jumps to the position, so it is constant time on average",
    "weight": 4
   },
   {
    "point": "Collisions happen when two keys map to the same slot",
    "weight": 3
   },
   {
    "point": "Collisions are handled by chaining or probing",
    "weight": 3
   },
   {
    "point": "Many collisions make lookups slower",
    "weight": 2
   }
  ],
  "key_points_source": "authored",
  "follow_ups": [
   "What happens if two keys give the same hash?",
   "Why must the hash of a key not change while it is in the map?"
  ],
